<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Otto of Greece · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was a Bavarian prince, son of King Ludwig I, chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-conference-of-1832">London Conference of 1832</a> as the first king of modern Greece. He ruled at first through Bavarian regents and resisted constitutional limits until the revolt of 1843 forced him to grant a constitution. A further uprising deposed him in 1862, and he died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/otto-of-greece.jpg" alt="Otto of Greece" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Dürck · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prinz_Otto_von_Bayern_Koenig_von_Griechenland_1833.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto was born on 1 June 1815 at Schloss Mirabell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, the second son of Crown Prince Ludwig of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, a prominent philhellene who gave financial aid to the Greek cause during the War of Independence. As a teenager he was tutored in Classical Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> by the philhellene scholar Friedrich Thiersch, who first suggested him as a candidate for the Greek throne. The London Protocol of 1829 required that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> be a monarchy under a prince not drawn from the ruling families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> was chosen but rejected the crown, and after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannis-kapodistrias">Ioannis Kapodistrias</a> in 1831 the Great Powers met in London and offered it to the 17-year-old Otto.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Otto's reign is usually divided into the regency of 1832 to 1835, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> from 1835 to 1843 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> from 1843 to 1862. His title was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, not King of the Hellenes, since the latter would have implied a claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> still under Ottoman rule. He arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nafplio</a> with 3,500 Bavarian troops and was welcomed warmly, wearing Greek costume and using the name Othon. The regency council of Bavarian officials under Count Josef Ludwig von Armansperg imposed financial stringency, and many Greeks saw its government as a foreign bureaucracy, the Bavarocracy. Otto moved the capital from Nafplio to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, then a small town of about 4,000 to 5,000 people, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-and-kapodistrian-university-of-athens">University of Athens</a> was founded in 1837.</p><p>Otto married Duchess Amalia of Oldenburg in 1836, and the marriage produced no heir. As a Catholic he was viewed with suspicion by many pious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Greek politics turned on parties tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and he tried to balance them. On 3 September 1843 infantry led by Colonel Dimitris Kallergis and General Yiannis Makriyiannis gathered in front of the palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and refused to leave until he granted a constitution. The square was renamed Constitution Square. British naval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockades</a> in 1850 and 1854 damaged his standing, and in October 1862 he was deposed while in the countryside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> on 26 July 1867. He saw himself as an enlightened absolutist and established educational institutions and several state services, but could not resolve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'s poverty or the economic meddling of outside powers. Among the buildings of his reign are the Old Royal Palace, now the Greek Parliament Building, the National Library and the National Observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Because of Greek hostility to non-Greek rule, this side of his reign has received little historical attention.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto of Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1832 the great powers chose <b>Otto of Bavaria</b> as king, creating the Kingdom of Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-hellenic-republic">First Hellenic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mirabell Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.81,13.04&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Mirabell Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4854 489 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Otto of Greece"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 759h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 759h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.81,13.04&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
